<section class="cam-hero">
    <p class="cam-eyebrow">You scanned a Crimson Defense QR code</p>
    <h1>This could've been a malicious site.</h1>
    <p class="cam-hero-text">This one was ours and it's harmless. The next one might not be. Here's a three-step check that takes a couple of seconds.</p>
</section>

<section class="cam-steps">
    <h2 class="cam-visually-hidden">Three-step check</h2>

    <div class="cam-step">
        <span class="cam-step-label">Step 1</span>
        <h3>Point, don't tap.</h3>
        <p>Let your camera find the code. It shows you a preview and waits for you, so there's no rush to tap anything.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cam-step">
        <span class="cam-step-label">Step 2</span>
        <h3>Read the domain.</h3>
        <p>The owner is the name right before the first single slash. Everything before it can be made up.</p>
        <p class="cam-step-note">A link shortener (bit.ly, tinyurl, etc.) hides the domain entirely. If you can't read it, don't trust it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cam-step">
        <span class="cam-step-label">Step 3</span>
        <h3>Decide.</h3>
        <p>Only open it if the owner is who you expected. Never log in, pay, or install anything from a random code.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="cam-spot">
    <h2>Spot the fake</h2>
    <p class="cam-spot-intro">The underlined part is who really owns each site.</p>

    <table class="cam-table">
